[{:seon.task/name "an-outgoing-text-cites-what-it-names"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner wants one string parser run through what Seon says and reads, to pull in relevant context (2026-10-23). What exists: `seon.ref/parse` runs only on authored text (a task's plan, a wiki page, a docstring); an eval's printed answer, an error's text and a tool's output are never parsed, so an agent that sees `at seon.agent/error-map 3` or a stack frame must ask `(source seon.agent/error-map)` in a turn of its own. Measured 2026-10-23 on four error-shaped strings: parse already reads var names, keywords, `path#anchor` and `path:N` out of them (resolve-only, summary depth), and a four-space-indented stack frame (`    seon.agent/g (REPL:7)`) is read like any line, so no separate flat mode is needed for names (the log-line hypothesis did not reproduce; a position inside `(REPL:7)` is read as no citation, which `a-citation-can-carry-a-position` is about). Correct: at the one place an answer's text is made (`seon.ctx.wire/tool-text`, which `seon.repl/text-of` calls; a record keeps data and no printed text), run `seon.ref/parse` over that text and resolve its citations on the agent's db with `seon.ref/references`, the same resolution a task's plan gets, and show each hit at summary depth by `seon.ref/shown` under a budget (the key this plan first named is gone; a new key, if one is needed, goes in default.edn). It reuses the one parser and the one resolver: no second grammar, no per-kind code (the error's own :seon.error/in and :seon.error/at stay data, this reads unstructured text). A name that resolves to nothing is silent here (resolve-only), never an error. Replaces: the agent's follow-up `(source ..)` and `(doc ..)` turns for what the text already names; measured as turns to green on the e2e tasks before and after. Cost proof (work done, never time): per outgoing text, the contracted calls and bytes are linear in its characters and its candidate names, plus one index seek per resolved name; n = 100 names, and a text with no names costs one scan. Proofs: an item whose text names a var cites it; an unresolved name adds nothing; the cost proof. Wiki: references.md (a new section) and render.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.repl-test/an-item-cites-the-vars-its-text-names seon.ref-test/parsing-an-outgoing-text-is-linear-in-its-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-citation-can-carry-a-position"
  :seon.task/plan "WAITS FOR OWNER RULING: new syntax (Root's gate #4); do not build before it. Idea: `at ns/f 3:6` reads today as the bare var `ns/f` and the position is dropped. Let a citation of a var carry the form-relative line and column that `:seon.error/at` already states (`(source v)` coordinates, 1-based) so context can show the var's recorded source with that line marked. Gate answers to bring the owner: (1) which existing mechanism does this: none, `path:N` is files only and marked stale, `seon.ref` has no position in a name; (2) v1: none; (3) the field: stack-frame `file:line` and compiler `file:line:col` are universal, but ours are form-relative on purpose; (4) why the error's own data cannot do it: it can for errors (`:seon.error/in`, `:seon.error/at`), this task is for text that is not an error map (a log, a plan, an agent's note). Plan if approved: one rule in `seon.ref/scan` beside `path:N`, a citation key for the position, a view that marks the line; positions stay out of stored facts (nothing stored depends on line numbers). Proof: ``seon.ref-test/a-var-with-a-position-shows-its-line``. Deletes nothing; do not add it if only error maps carry positions."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ref/an-outgoing-text-cites-what-it-names"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/a-var-with-a-position-shows-its-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-resolver-names-a-thing"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: the agent tools take a name as a symbol, keyword, var, string or namespace and each decides which: seon.doc/named-under? strips a colon from text (7d299862fd, 92a4f76dd9, 0a58e8aa31); my.agent's ns-named? (4e8ffa1112) serves callers and callees but not lines or source, each with its own cond; prove's names are sniffed by qualification (twice a silent ran 0); seon.doc/ns-hit decides a test by the -test suffix though :var/meta holding :test is stored; example agents are found by the example/ prefix in three places. The change: one resolver in seon.ref, the one parser of citations, answering a name tagged by kind (var, ns, schema, section, task) or a no-var answer; every tool takes its answer, and :ns filters compare resolved namespace symbols, never text. Docs: docs/references.md and docs/agent.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/every-tool-names-a-thing-the-same-way}}
]
